Question 6. Online Shopping System

An Online Shopping System maintains a list of products available for purchase. Each product has a product ID, product name, and price. The system uses an ArrayList to store and manage product details.

(a) Create a class ProductDetails with data members productId, productName, and price and make a constructor to initialize all variables.

(b) In the main() method, create an ArrayList of ProductDetails objects and add details of all three given products using the add() method.

(c) Display all the products stored in the ArrayList using for-each loop.

(d) Use the remove() method to remove one product from the ArrayList and display the updated list.

Answer:

import java.util.ArrayList;

class ProductDetails {

int productId;
String productName;
int price;

ProductDetails(int productId, String productName, int price) {
    this.productId = productId;
    this.productName = productName;
    this.price = price;
}

}

public class Main {
public static void main(String[] args) {

    ProductDetails p1 = new ProductDetails(1, "Soap", 100);
    ProductDetails p2 = new ProductDetails(2, "Gel", 300);
    ProductDetails p3 = new ProductDetails(3, "Shampoo", 400);

    ArrayList<ProductDetails> products = new ArrayList<>();

    products.add(p1);
    products.add(p2);
    products.add(p3);

    System.out.println("All Products:");

    for (ProductDetails p : products) {
        System.out.println(
            p.productId + " " +
            p.productName + " " +
            p.price
        );
    }

    products.remove(p2);

    System.out.println("Updated List:");

    for (ProductDetails p : products) {
        System.out.println(
            p.productId + " " +
            p.productName + " " +
            p.price
        );
    }
}

}

Output:

All Products:
1 Soap 100
2 Gel 300
3 Shampoo 400

Updated List:
1 Soap 100
3 Shampoo 400

Question 7. College Event Registration System

A College Event Registration System maintains the names of students registered for a technical event. A student should be registered only once.

(a) Select a suitable Java Collection to store the names of registered students such that duplicate names are not allowed.

(b) Add at least four students to the collection.

(c) Display the total number of registered students using the size() method.

(d) Remove one student from the collection.

(e) Write a suitable loop to display all the registered student names.

Answer:

The suitable collection for this problem is HashSet because HashSet does not allow duplicate elements.

import java.util.HashSet;

public class Main {
public static void main(String[] args) {

    HashSet<String> students = new HashSet<>();

    students.add("Rahul");
    students.add("Aman");
    students.add("Priya");
    students.add("Neha");

    students.add("Rahul");

    System.out.println("Total Students: " + students.size());

    students.remove("Aman");

    System.out.println("Registered Students:");

    for (String student : students) {
        System.out.println(student);
    }
}

}

Output:

Total Students: 4

Registered Students:
Rahul
Priya
Neha

HashSet is used because it automatically prevents duplicate student names. The duplicate name "Rahul" is not added again. The size() method returns the total number of unique students. The remove() method removes a student from the collection and the for-each loop is used to display all registered students.

Question 8. Food Order Management System

A Food Order Management System maintains a list of food items selected by customers. New food items can be added at the beginning of the order list, while the last food item can be removed when required.

(a) Create a LinkedList to store the names of food items.

(b) Add the following food items at the beginning of the list: "Pizza", "Burger", "Pasta", and "Sandwich".

(c) Display the first item of the list.

(d) Remove one food item from the end of the list.

(e) Write a suitable loop to display all the food items currently present in the list.

Answer:

import java.util.LinkedList;

public class FoodOrder {
public static void main(String[] args) {

    LinkedList<String> food = new LinkedList<>();

    food.addFirst("Pizza");
    food.addFirst("Burger");
    food.addFirst("Pasta");
    food.addFirst("Sandwich");

    System.out.println("First Item: " + food.getFirst());

    food.removeLast();

    System.out.println("Food Items:");

    for (String item : food) {
